<?nvs
// Loose input read as a declared shape, which is the only way a request's
// values reach a program with types on them.
//
// This leg runs under `nvs run --request examples/input-shapes.nvsr`, so the
// form and the query string below are a real request's: the file beside this
// one describes it in the format `crates/nvs-test/src/request.rs` owns.
// Without the `--request` argument the first line is `Core\Request::postAs`
// refusing, which is `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`.
//
// There is no shaped `array<T>` and no per-key-typed array: an array stays
// homogeneous (`rule:types/arrays`), and the conversion happens once, at the
// boundary, where a failure is still a `400` rather than a surprise three
// calls deeper. After it nothing downstream is holding a `mixed` to check.

// The submitted form, read as the type written at the call site. Each named
// field is converted with `as` (`rule:types/conversion`), so `age` is an `int`
// here rather than the text the wire carried.
//
// `tainted {…}` is one word over the whole shape rather than a `tainted` on
// every text field: it distributes to each text-carrying field and is gone
// before the checker sees it (`rule:security/tainted-qualifier`), so `email`
// is a `tainted string` and `age`, being an `int`, carries nothing. That is
// what a peer chose, said out loud — the qualifier costs nothing at run time
// and is the reason a sink cannot be reached from here by accident.
(tainted {email: string, age: int, nickname?: string}) $account =
    Core\Request::postAs<tainted {email: string, age: int, nickname?: string}>();
echo 'email is ', $account->email, "\n";
echo 'age is ', $account->age as string, "\n";

// `{nickname?: string}` proves the field's type and not its presence, so an
// absent key is a catchable throw and `??` is the spelling that asks without
// one (`rule:types/shape-type`). Optional is not nullable: `{nickname: ?string}`
// would demand the key and let it hold `null`.
echo 'nickname is ', $account->nickname ?? 'absent', "\n";

// The query string, through the same walk. `queryAs` and `postAs` are one
// mechanism over two subjects — `Core\Arr::shapeAs` below is that mechanism
// said in general, with the array named outright.
(tainted {city: string}) $where = Core\Request::queryAs<tainted {city: string}>();
echo 'city is ', $where->city, "\n";

// Width subtyping over loose values: `note` is a key the shape never named, so
// it is left behind rather than refused. That is deliberate and has no opt-out
// — a client adding a field must not break a server that never asked for it
// (`rule:core-api/shape-rules`).
if (Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{city: string}>(['city' => 'Vienna', 'note' => 'ignored']))
    == "{\"city\":\"Vienna\"}") {
    echo "an unnamed field was left behind\n";
}

// The failure, which is the half a form actually needs: `{name: "bad"}` reads
// the one bracket-named subtree under `bad`, where `age` holds text no `as int`
// accepts and `city` was never sent at all. Both are reported, each at its own
// dotted path, because a form wants the whole list rather than the first item
// of it (`rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`).
try {
    Core\Request::queryAs<tainted {age: int, city: string}>({ name: 'bad' });
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $refused) {
    if (Core\Arr::count($refused->issues) == 2) {
        echo 'two fields did not fit: ',
            $refused->issues['0']->path, ', ', $refused->issues['1']->path, "\n";
    }
}
